---
source: crates/togi/tests/cli/mod.rs
expression: "help(\"emergency-stop\")"
---
Internal emergency recovery that stops leftover trials and sets every core to offset 0

Internal recovery for a togi process that died with trial workloads or Curve Optimizer offsets left behind; the services run it after an abnormal exit. It requires Linux and root. It tries to take the host lock, validates the CPU, the SMU driver and the core topology, stops every stale togi trial scope and confirms none is left. Only then does it write 0 to every core and read every core back.

It accepts no offset or configuration and never opens the session journal. A foreign-owned or nonregular lock entry (including a symlink) does not prevent this zero-only recovery. Exit status 0 means every core read back 0 under the lock; 4 means every core read back 0 but exclusion from another writer was not proven. A busy lock still leaves offsets unchanged and exits 3. A failed validation or slot mapping or an unconfirmed sweep still blocks zeroing. Other refusals and failures exit 1, with the cause on stderr.

Usage: togi emergency-stop [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --state-dir <path>
          State directory
          
          [default: /var/lib/togi]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Examples:
  sudo togi emergency-stop   Stop leftover trials, then set every core to 0 and read each back
